[
;;; Blockchain chain communities and fan clubs (2026-07-26, owner request).
;;; ADR-2607257200.
;;;
;;; These two clusters share a counting structure that no other part of the
;;; corpus has in such a pure form: the size is INFERRED FROM AN ARTIFACT the
;;; community leaves behind — on-chain addresses, issued membership numbers —
;;; rather than published by anyone. Both artifacts systematically overstate.
;;; The proxy and its bias are recorded in :org/proxy-metric and :org/proxy-bias
;;; so no reader mistakes an artifact count for a headcount.

{:org/id :org-global-chain-communities
 :org/name "Blockchain chain communities (Ethereum, Bitcoin, Solana, Cosmos zones, L2 ecosystems)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-chain-community
 :org/category :cat-online
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :global
 :org/region-primary :region-north-america
 :org/countries ["US" "DE" "SG" "KR" "NG" "BR" "IN" "JP" "CH" "AE"]
 :org/founding-date 2009
 :org/member-count 45000000
 :org/member-count-basis "source-pinned as an ESTIMATE DERIVED FROM A PROXY, not a roll: a16z's methodology puts real monthly active crypto users at roughly 30-60 million, against about 220 million monthly ACTIVE ADDRESSES — i.e. only ~14-27% of active addresses correspond to a distinct person once bots and sybils are filtered, an overstatement factor of roughly 4-7x. The corpus records the midpoint (~45M) with that derivation stated, and asserts no cumulative 'total users' figure at all, since cumulative unique addresses are larger again and even less meaningful."
 :org/proxy-metric "unique / active on-chain addresses"
 :org/proxy-bias "Overstates people by ~4-7x: one person holds many addresses; airdrop farming manufactures addresses deliberately; contracts and bots transact. It also UNDERSTATES in the opposite direction where an exchange custodies millions of users behind a handful of addresses. The error is therefore not a simple scale factor and cannot be inverted reliably. LayerZero's CEO publicly estimated that only 400,000-600,000 of 6 million pre-airdrop accounts were real — a 10x inflation in that instance."
 :org/legal-registration :unregistered
 :org/registration-note "The chain community as such has no legal person anywhere. Foundations (Ethereum Foundation, Solana Foundation) are registered and fund development but do not constitute or represent the community; validators, holders and local meetup chapters are unaffiliated with them."
 :org/governance-model :meritocratic
 :org/mission-statement "The people who run nodes, validate, hold governance tokens, write clients, translate documentation and organise local meetups around a single ledger — a community whose boundary is participation in one shared state machine, and whose apparent size is an artefact of how addresses are counted."
 :org/coverage-note "Distinct from the DAO entry, which covers treasuries and on-chain voting inside a chain. This entry is the chain's own community layer."
 :org/related-org "org-global-daos"
 :org/source-name "a16z crypto, Estimating the number of real crypto users"
 :org/source-url "https://a16zcrypto.com/posts/article/estimating-crypto-users/"
 :org/source-date "2025"
 :org/source-tier :secondary
 :org/source-caveat "a16z is an investor in this sector; its estimate is the most methodologically explicit one located, not a disinterested one."
 :org/pinned-on "2026-07-26"
 :org/data-provenance "source-pinned"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-global-validator-node-communities
 :org/name "Validator, miner and node-operator communities"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-chain-community
 :org/category :cat-online
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :global
 :org/region-primary :region-europe
 :org/countries ["US" "DE" "FI" "SG" "KZ" "PY" "IS" "CA"]
 :org/founding-date 2009
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown as people — validator and node counts are directly observable on-chain (Ethereum's validator count is published continuously, Bitcoin's reachable nodes are crawled), but one operator runs many validators and staking services run tens of thousands, so the machine count is not an operator count. This is the inverse of the address problem: here the artefact UNDERSTATES the community when read as people, because concentration hides operators."
 :org/proxy-metric "validator / node counts observed on-chain or by crawlers"
 :org/proxy-bias "Machines, not people. Staking pools and hosting providers concentrate many validators under one operator, so validator counts overstate independence while understating nothing about scale; reachable-node crawls miss nodes behind firewalls."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/registration-note "Large staking operators are registered companies; solo stakers and hobby node runners are individuals with no organizational form, and the community that connects them is a chat channel and a client-team mailing list."
 :org/governance-model :meritocratic
 :org/mission-statement "Operators who keep a chain running — solo stakers, mining pools, RPC providers, client teams — organised through client-team calls, testnets and incident channels, and the group that actually executes a hard fork when governance decides on one."
 :org/related-org "org-global-chain-communities"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

